From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 23:08:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] m68k: movmemsi (memmove) copies overlapping blocks the
 right way

Since GCC 10, the movmem optab is memmove: the blocks may overlap. The
expander shared cpymemsi's forward copy and only switched to a backward
copy when both blocks had the same base and constant offsets in MEM_EXPR.
Anything else was copied forwards, so

    for (i = n; i > 0; --i) v[i] = v[i - 1];      (loop distribution)
    __builtin_memmove (a + 1, a, 64);
    std::copy_backward (a, a + 16, a + 17);

with a constant size smeared a[0] over the block at -O1, -O2 and -Os.

movmemsi now copies backwards when the destination is proved to lie
inside the source (same base register, or the same base followed through
SSA pointer arithmetic), forwards when that is proved safe, and otherwise
compares the addresses at run time and runs a forward or a backward copy.
At -Os an unknown direction calls memmove instead. A backward copy moves
the odd trailing bytes first, so its longs stay aligned as a forward
copy's are (68000). cpymemsi (memcpy) is unchanged.
---
