+   Since GCC 10, "movmem" means memmove: the blocks may overlap.  Loop
+   distribution turns an overlapping "shift up" loop such as
+     for (i = n; i > 0; --i) v[i] = v[i - 1];
+   into memmove (v + 1, v, n * sizeof *v), and std::copy_backward does the same
+   through __builtin_memmove.  A forward copy then smears v[0] over the whole
+   block.  So a memmove is copied forwards only when that is proved safe,
+   backwards when the destination is proved to lie inside the source, and
+   otherwise the direction is chosen at run time (or, at -Os, memmove is
+   called).  */
